1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:04,100
Hi, we're Team BTYECODE, and this is

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:06,520
Paradox, our idea for the security

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,480
track of VoyageHack.

4
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:11,340
Here's the thing about B2B travel.

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,360
The bugs that cost real money

6
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:15,080
don't look like bugs.

7
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,040
A refund that pays out twice?

8
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:19,800
The server says 200 OK.

9
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,380
Same with a fare the agent sets

10
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:23,540
themselves, or one agency reading

11
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:25,540
another agency's passports.

12
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:28,230
Scanners look for bad inputs.

13
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:29,920
We look for outcomes that

14
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:31,540
should be impossible.

15
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,480
Paradox starts with just what TBO gives us:

16
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,880
a staging link, a few test accounts,

17
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:39,820
some recorded traffic and logs.

18
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:40,980
No API docs.

19
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,400
From that, it works out the routes, how

20
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,220
people actually book, who owns what, and

21
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:47,840
where the money sits.

22
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:50,560
Then it plays with those journeys the

23
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:51,800
way a sneaky user would.

24
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,240
Cancel twice.

25
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:54,260
Skip the payment.

26
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,760
Set your own fare.

27
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:57,620
Pretend to be another agency.

28
00:00:58,085 --> 00:01:01,730
After each try, it checks 12 simple rules.

29
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:03,620
Your wallet can't end up richer.

30
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,680
The same booking can't get cheaper.

31
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,560
One agency never sees another's data.

32
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:11,100
If a rule breaks, that's a finding.

33
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:12,785
If not, it stays quiet.

34
00:01:12,805 --> 00:01:13,605
Like this one.

35
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:15,160
Cancel a booking that's already

36
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:19,080
cancelled, and the wallet gains ₹7,776.

37
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,225
Every single time.

38
00:01:21,245 --> 00:01:24,720
Paradox ran it 3 times to be sure, and

39
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:27,380
cut it down to just 4 requests.

40
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:31,253
Every booking it creates carries fake details:

41
00:01:31,273 --> 00:01:34,900
a made-up name, email and passport.

42
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,580
If those turn up in another agency's

43
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,200
account, the leak proves itself.

44
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:41,800
Each finding turns into a ticket for

45
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,820
the team that owns that code, with what

46
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:47,100
it costs in rupees, the exact steps,

47
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:49,280
and how to fix it.

48
00:01:49,725 --> 00:01:51,560
And it keeps watching.

49
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:53,760
When a new release went out, Paradox

50
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,520
tested the changed part first.

51
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:58,160
The new amend feature trusted

52
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:00,860
whatever total the client sent.

53
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:02,500
It was flagged as critical

54
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:04,800
about 12 seconds later.

55
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:08,740
Once the fix shipped, Paradox re-ran

56
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:11,100
its proofs, and the fixed tickets

57
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:12,780
closed on their own.

58
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,140
Along the way, it maps every endpoint, even

59
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:19,200
an old v1 route nobody remembered, which

60
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:21,380
was still leaking passports.

61
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:23,060
Does it work?

62
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:25,920
We planted 172 random bugs.

63
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:28,180
Paradox found all of them, with

64
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:29,800
zero false alarms.

65
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,757
A regular scanner found about 30%.

66
00:02:33,770 --> 00:02:35,990
We also tried it on an airline

67
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:38,430
platform it had never seen.

68
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:40,990
After two small, general fixes, it

69
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:43,770
caught all 7 planted bugs, and stayed

70
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:46,010
silent on the clean version.

71
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:47,663
And it's careful.

72
00:02:47,683 --> 00:02:50,330
It stays in scope, only touches its own

73
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:52,830
test bookings, slows down if the server

74
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:55,890
struggles, has a kill switch, and logs

75
00:02:55,910 --> 00:02:57,410
every request.

76
00:02:57,830 --> 00:02:59,270
That's Paradox.

77
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:01,090
It catches the bug that makes money

78
00:03:01,110 --> 00:03:03,430
appear, before someone else does.

79
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:05,210
Thank you.
